                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  27A0'                         EXADDR:
  27A0'   AF                    	XRA	A
  27A1'   32 2DEE'              	STA	SRNGF
  27A4'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27A5'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  27A7'   CA 27D0'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  27AA'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  27AB'   CD 27E5'              	CALL	EXNUM
  27AE'   DA 27CE'              	JC	EXA_NO
  27B1'   22 2DEF'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  27B4'   22 2DF1'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  27B7'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  27B8'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  27BA'   C2 27C7'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  27BD'   13                    	INX	D
  27BE'   CD 27E5'              	CALL	EXNUM
  27C1'   DA 27CE'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  27C4'   22 2DF1'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  27C7'                         EXA_YS:
  27C7'   EB                    	XCHG
  27C8'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27CA'   32 2DEE'              	STA	SRNGF
  27CD'   C9                    	RET
  27CE'                         EXA_NO:
  27CE'   EB                    	XCHG
  27CF'   C9                    	RET
  27D0'                         EXA_AL:
  27D0'   23                    	INX	H
  27D1'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27D2'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  27D5'   22 2DEF'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  27D8'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  27DB'   22 2DF1'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  27DE'   E1                    	POP	H
  27DF'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27E1'   32 2DEE'              	STA	SRNGF
  27E4'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  27E5'                         EXNUM:
  27E5'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  27E6'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  27E8'   D8                    	RC
  27E9'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  27EB'   3F                    	CMC
  27EC'   D8                    	RC
  27ED'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  27F0'                         EXN_LP:
  27F0'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  27F1'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  27F3'   DA 280D'              	JC	EXN_DN
  27F6'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  27F8'   D2 280D'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  27FB'   13                    	INX	D
  27FC'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  27FD'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  27FE'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  2800'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2801'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  2802'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  2803'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  2804'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  2805'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  2806'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  2807'   D1                    	POP	D
  2808'   09                    	DAD	B
  2809'   C1                    	POP	B
  280A'   C3 27F0'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  280D'                         EXN_DN:
  280D'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  280E'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  280F'                         EX_S:
  280F'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2810'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2811'   CA 2853'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  2814'   32 2DF3'              	STA	SDELIM
  2817'   23                    	INX	H
  2818'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  281B'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  281E'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  281F'   CD 2859'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2822'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2823'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2824'   CA 2853'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  2827'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  282A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  282C'   32 2DBB'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  282F'   11 2E05'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2832'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2834'   CD 2859'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2837'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2838'   32 2E04'              	STA	SREPLN
  283B'   AF                    	XRA	A
  283C'   32 2DF4'              	STA	SGFLAG
  283F'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2840'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  2842'   C2 284B'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2845'   23                    	INX	H
  2846'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2848'   32 2DF4'              	STA	SGFLAG
  284B'                         EX_SG:
  284B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  284C'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  284D'   C2 25DA'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2850'   C3 2879'              	JMP	SUBGO
  2853'                         SUBNOP:
  2853'   21 26A2'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2856'   C3 25E8'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  2859'                         SCOPY:
  2859'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  285B'                         SCP_LP:
  285B'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  285C'   B9                    	CMP	C
  285D'   D2 2876'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2860'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2861'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2862'   CA 2876'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2865'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2866'   21 2DF3'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2869'   BE                    	CMP	M
  286A'   E1                    	POP	H
  286B'   CA 2875'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  286E'   12                    	STAX	D
  286F'   13                    	INX	D
  2870'   23                    	INX	H
  2871'   04                    	INR	B
  2872'   C3 285B'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2875'                         SCP_SK:
  2875'   23                    	INX	H
  2876'                         SCP_DN:
  2876'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2877'   12                    	STAX	D
  2878'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  2879'                         SUBGO:
  2879'   CD 125E'              	CALL	GZONE
  287C'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  287F'   22 2E02'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2882'   21 2DFF'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  2885'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  2888'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2889'   32 2DF5'              	STA	SANY
  288C'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  288F'   22 2DF7'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2892'   3A 2DEE'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2895'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2896'   CA 28A8'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  2899'   CD 2168'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  289C'   DA 2924'              	JC	SUBBAD
  289F'   2A 2DEF'              	LHLD	SRNG1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


  28A2'   CD 11CC'              	CALL	GOLN
  28A5'   C3 28AE'              	JMP	SUBLP
  28A8'                         SUB_1L:
  28A8'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  28AB'   22 2DF9'              	SHLD	SLINES
  28AE'                         SUBLP:
  28AE'   CD 292A'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  28B1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28B2'   CA 28C0'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  28B5'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28B7'   32 2DF5'              	STA	SANY
  28BA'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  28BD'   22 2DF7'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  28C0'                         SUB_NX:
  28C0'   2A 2DF9'              	LHLD	SLINES
  28C3'   2B                    	DCX	H
  28C4'   22 2DF9'              	SHLD	SLINES
  28C7'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  28C8'   B5                    	ORA	L
  28C9'   CA 28E7'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  28CC'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  28CF'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28D0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  28D3'   3A 2DF5'              	LDA	SANY
  28D6'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28D7'   CA 28E1'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  28DA'   2A 2DF7'              	LHLD	SBACK
  28DD'   23                    	INX	H
  28DE'   22 2DF7'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  28E1'                         SUB_AE:
  28E1'   CD 0A68'              	CALL	ATEOF
  28E4'   D2 28AE'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  28E7'                         SUBDON:
  28E7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  28EA'   3A 2DF5'              	LDA	SANY
  28ED'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28EE'   CA 290C'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  28F1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28F3'   32 2D2F'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  28F6'   2A 2DF7'              	LHLD	SBACK
  28F9'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  28FA'   B5                    	ORA	L
  28FB'   CA 2906'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  28FE'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2900'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  2903'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  2906'                         SUB_PL:
  2906'   CD 1276'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  2909'   C3 07C1'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-127


                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  290C'                         SUBNFD:
  290C'   21 2DFF'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  290F'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  2912'   2A 2E02'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  2915'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  2918'   21 2672'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  291B'   CD 25E8'              	CALL	EXERR
  291E'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2921'   C3 25E3'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  2924'                         SUBBAD:
  2924'   21 2660'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  2927'   C3 25DD'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  292A'                         SUBLIN:
  292A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  292B'   32 2DF6'              	STA	SLCHG
  292E'   CD 29A9'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2931'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  2934'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  2934'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  2937'   DA 293F'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  293A'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  293C'   C2 2934'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  293F'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  293F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2942'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2943'   CD 181C'              	CALL	CEND
  2946'   D1                    	POP	D
  2947'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  294A'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  294B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  294E'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  294F'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2950'   FA 29A2'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  2953'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  2954'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  2955'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2958'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  295B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


  295E'   DA 29A2'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2961'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  2964'   EB                    	XCHG
  2965'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  2968'   EB                    	XCHG
  2969'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  296C'   EB                    	XCHG
  296D'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  296E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2971'   D1                    	POP	D
  2972'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2973'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  2976'   3A 2DF5'              	LDA	SANY
  2979'   B7                    	ORA	A
  297A'   C2 2987'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  297D'   3A 2DF6'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2980'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2981'   C2 2987'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2984'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2987'                         SUBL_C:
  2987'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  298A'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  298B'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  298D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2990'   11 2E04'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2993'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  2996'                         SUBL_I:
  2996'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2998'   32 2DF6'              	STA	SLCHG
  299B'   3A 2DF4'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  299E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  299F'   C2 293F'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  29A2'                         SUBL_D:
  29A2'   CD 29A9'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  29A5'   3A 2DF6'              	LDA	SLCHG
  29A8'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  29A9'                         SUBBOL:
  29A9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  29AC'   CD 0624'              	CALL	BOL
  29AF'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  29B2'                         VC_SRCH:
  29B2'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  29B5'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  29B6'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  29B9'   22 2DE4'              	SHLD	SCNT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-129


  29BC'   C1                    	POP	B
  29BD'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  29BE'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  29C0'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  29C2'   CA 29C7'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  29C5'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  29C7'                         VS_DIR:
  29C7'   32 2DB9'              	STA	SDIR
  29CA'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  29CD'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  29D0'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  29D1'   CD 1F11'              	CALL	RDLINE
  29D4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29D5'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  29D6'   3A 2D90'              	LDA	RLLEN
  29D9'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29DA'   CA 29EF'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  29DD'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  29DE'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  29E0'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  29E3'   19                    	DAD	D
  29E4'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  29E6'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  29E7'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  29EA'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29EC'   32 2DBB'              	STA	SHAVE
  29EF'                         VS_OLD:
  29EF'   3A 2DBB'              	LDA	SHAVE
  29F2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29F3'   CA 29FF'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  29F6'   3A 2DB9'              	LDA	SDIR
  29F9'   32 2DBA'              	STA	SDIRE
  29FC'   C3 2A28'              	JMP	SRCHN
  29FF'                         VS_NOP:
  29FF'   21 26A2'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2A02'   C3 25E8'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2A05'                         VC_N:
  2A05'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2A08'   3A 2DB9'              	LDA	SDIR
  2A0B'   C3 2A15'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2A0E'                         VCB_N:
  2A0E'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2A11'   3A 2DB9'              	LDA	SDIR
  2A14'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  2A15'                         VN_GO:
  2A15'   32 2DBA'              	STA	SDIRE
  2A18'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2A1B'   22 2DE4'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A1E'   3A 2DBB'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2A21'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A22'   CA 29FF'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  2A25'   C3 2A28'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-130


                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2A28'                         SRCHN:
  2A28'   CD 125E'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2A2B'   CD 06F4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
  2A2E'                         SN_LP:
  2A2E'   CD 2A63'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2A31'   DA 2A4E'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2A34'   2A 2DE4'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2A37'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2A38'   22 2DE4'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A3B'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2A3C'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2A3D'   C2 2A2E'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2A40'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2A43'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2A46'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A48'   32 2DC6'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2A4B'   C3 1276'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2A4E'                         SN_BAD:
  2A4E'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2A51'   DA 0749'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2A54'   CD 073C'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2A57'   21 2672'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2A5A'   CD 25E8'              	CALL	EXERR
  2A5D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2A60'   C3 25E3'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2A63'                         SRCHGO:
  2A63'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A65'   32 2DED'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  2A68'   CD 2A7E'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2A6B'   D0                    	RNC
  2A6C'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2A6F'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2A70'   3A 2DBA'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2A73'   2F                    	CMA
  2A74'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2A77'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A78'   32 2DED'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2A7B'   C3 2A7E'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-131


                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2A7E'                         SRCHW:
  2A7E'   3A 2DBA'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2A81'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2A84'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2A87'   22 2D2C'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2A8A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A8B'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2A8E'                         SW_LP:
  2A8E'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  2A91'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2A92'   CD 2A9E'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2A95'   D0                    	RNC
  2A96'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2A99'   D2 2A8E'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2A9C'   37                    	STC
  2A9D'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2A9E'                         SRCH1:
  2A9E'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2AA1'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2AA4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AA5'   FA 2AC9'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2AA8'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2AAB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2AAE'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2AB1'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2AB2'   3A 2DED'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2AB5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AB6'   CA 2ABF'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2AB9'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2ABA'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2ABB'   37                    	STC
  2ABC'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2ABD'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2ABE'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2ABF'                         SR1_FW:
  2ABF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2AC2'   D8                    	RC
  2AC3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2AC6'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2AC9'                         SR1_BK:
  2AC9'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2ACC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2ACF'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2AD2'   D8                    	RC
  2AD3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2AD6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-132


  2AD9'   D8                    	RC
  2ADA'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2ADD'                         MSGNAM:
  2ADD'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2ADE'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2AE0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2AE3'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AE4'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2AE5'   23                    	INX	H
  2AE6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2AE7'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2AE9'   C2 2AF6'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2AEC'   21 2710'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2AEF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2AF2'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AF3'   C3 2B40'              	JMP	MN_END
  2AF6'                         MN_HAS:
  2AF6'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2AF8'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2AFB'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AFC'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2AFD'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2AFE'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2AFF'   CA 2B0D'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2B02'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B03'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2B05'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B08'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2B0A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B0D'                         MN_NAM:
  2B0D'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B0E'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B0F'   23                    	INX	H
  2B10'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-133


  2B12'                         MN_NM:
  2B12'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B13'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B15'   CA 2B20'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2B18'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B1B'   23                    	INX	H
  2B1C'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2B1D'   C2 2B12'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2B20'                         MN_EXT:
  2B20'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B21'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2B24'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2B25'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B26'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B28'   CA 2B40'              	JZ	MN_END
  2B2B'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2B2D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B30'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2B32'                         MN_XL:
  2B32'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B33'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B35'   CA 2B40'              	JZ	MN_END
  2B38'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B3B'   23                    	INX	H
  2B3C'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2B3D'   C2 2B32'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2B40'                         MN_END:
  2B40'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2B42'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2B45'                         MSGFLG:
  2B45'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2B47'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B48'   CA 2B58'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2B4B'   3A 2D2F'              	LDA	MODF
  2B4E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B4F'   CA 2B58'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2B52'   21 271A'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2B55'   CD 2B6D'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2B58'                         MF_RO:
  2B58'   3A 2DA6'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2B5B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B5C'   CA 2B65'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2B5F'   21 2725'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2B62'   CD 2B6D'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2B65'                         MF_NEW:
  2B65'   3A 2DA4'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2B68'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B69'   C8                    	RZ
  2B6A'   21 2730'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2B6D'                         MF_PUT:
  2B6D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-134


  2B6E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B6F'   C2 2B7B'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2B72'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2B74'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B75'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2B77'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B7A'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B7B'                         MF_STR:
  2B7B'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2B7E'                         EMITW:
  2B7E'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2B7F'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2B80'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2B82'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2B85'   CD 2BA3'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B88'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2B8B'   CD 2BA3'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B8E'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2B91'   CD 2BA3'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B94'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2B97'   CD 2BA3'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B9A'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2B9B'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2B9D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BA0'   D1                    	POP	D
  2BA1'   C1                    	POP	B
  2BA2'   C9                    	RET
  2BA3'                         EWDIG:
  2BA3'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2BA5'                         EW_LP:
  2BA5'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2BA6'   93                    	SUB	E
  2BA7'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2BA8'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2BA9'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2BAA'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2BAB'   DA 2BB2'              	JC	EW_DN
  2BAE'   0C                    	INR	C
  2BAF'   C3 2BA5'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2BB2'                         EW_DN:
  2BB2'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2BB3'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2BB4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BB5'   C2 2BBB'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2BB8'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2BB9'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BBA'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2BBB'                         EW_PR:
  2BBB'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2BBC'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2BBE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BC1'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-135


  2BC3'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2BC4'                         ISEMPT:
  2BC4'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2BC7'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2BC8'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2BC9'   C0                    	RNZ
  2BCA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2BCD'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2BCE'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2BCF'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2BD0'                         MSGCTG:
  2BD0'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2BD3'   CD 2ADD'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2BD6'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2BD8'   CD 2B45'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2BDB'   CD 2BC4'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2BDE'   C2 2BE7'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2BE1'   21 2743'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2BE4'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2BE7'                         MG_POS:
  2BE7'   21 2736'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2BEA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BED'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2BF0'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2BF1'   CD 2B7E'              	CALL	EMITW
  2BF4'   21 273D'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2BF7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BFA'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2BFD'   23                    	INX	H
  2BFE'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BFF'   CD 2B7E'              	CALL	EMITW
  2C02'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2C05'   23                    	INX	H
  2C06'   D1                    	POP	D
  2C07'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2C0A'   C8                    	RZ
  2C0B'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2C0C'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2C0E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C11'   E1                    	POP	H
  2C12'   C3 2B7E'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2C15'                         FILMSG:
  2C15'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2C18'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-136


  2C1A'   C8                    	RZ
  2C1B'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2C1E'   CD 2ADD'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C21'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2C22'   C3 2B45'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2C25'                         WRMSG:
  2C25'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2C28'   CD 2ADD'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C2B'   21 2755'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2C2E'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2C31'                         MSGPST:
  2C31'   3A 2DA3'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2C34'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C35'   C8                    	RZ
  2C36'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2C37'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2C38'   32 2DA3'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2C3B'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C3C'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2C3E'   CA 2C5A'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2C41'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2C43'   CA 2C60'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2C46'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  2C48'   CA 2C51'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2C4B'   CD 2BD0'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2C4E'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C51'                         MP_BIG:
  2C51'   21 26F1'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2C54'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2C57'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C5A'                         MP_FIL:
  2C5A'   CD 2C15'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2C5D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C60'                         MP_WRT:
  2C60'   CD 2C25'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2C63'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2C66'                         VC_CTLG:
  2C66'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2C68'   32 2DA3'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2C6B'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2C6D'   32 2D30'              	STA	RINTENT
  2C70'   C3 06CB'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2C73'                         CMDTAB:
  2C73'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-137


  2C74'   2C66'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2C76'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2C77'   07FE'                 	DW	VC_H
  2C79'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2C7A'   0828'                 	DW	VC_L
  2C7C'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2C7D'   08F9'                 	DW	VC_J
  2C7F'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2C80'   090B'                 	DW	VC_K
  2C82'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2C83'   085B'                 	DW	VC_0
  2C85'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2C86'   0879'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2C88'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2C89'   08B7'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2C8B'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2C8C'   08DB'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2C8E'   77                    	DB	'w'
  2C8F'   0AEA'                 	DW	VC_W
  2C91'   62                    	DB	'b'
  2C92'   0B3B'                 	DW	VC_B
  2C94'   65                    	DB	'e'
  2C95'   0CD1'                 	DW	VC_E
  2C97'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2C98'   0AF1'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2C9A'   42                    	DB	'B'
  2C9B'   0B42'                 	DW	VCBGB
  2C9D'   45                    	DB	'E'
  2C9E'   0CD8'                 	DW	VCBGE
  2CA0'   47                    	DB	'G'
  2CA1'   119C'                 	DW	VC_G
  2CA3'   67                    	DB	'g'
  2CA4'   11EF'                 	DW	VC_LG
  2CA6'   78                    	DB	'x'
  2CA7'   17D3'                 	DW	VC_X
  2CA9'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  2CAA'   1F84'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  2CAC'   66                    	DB	'f'
  2CAD'   021A'                 	DW	VC_FF
  2CAF'   46                    	DB	'F'
  2CB0'   021F'                 	DW	VC_FB
  2CB2'   74                    	DB	't'
  2CB3'   0224'                 	DW	VC_TF
  2CB5'   54                    	DB	'T'
  2CB6'   0229'                 	DW	VC_TB
  2CB8'   3B                    	DB	';'
  2CB9'   0240'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
  2CBB'   2C                    	DB	','
  2CBC'   024A'                 	DW	VC_COMMA
  2CBE'   79                    	DB	'y'
  2CBF'   1ABC'                 	DW	VC_Y
  2CC1'   59                    	DB	'Y'
  2CC2'   1AC4'                 	DW	VY_YY
  2CC4'   70                    	DB	'p'
  2CC5'   1CFA'                 	DW	VC_P
  2CC7'   50                    	DB	'P'
